Subject: engine: packed arrays: the array builtins read, write and move the packed storage in place (QJS_PACKED_ARRAYS)

What this does
  Every array builtin that can keep a number array in its packed kind does so, and the others take
  the generic route unchanged:

  Reads. includes/indexOf/lastIndexOf scan the raw storage (js_arr_packed_find: strict equality for
  indexOf/lastIndexOf, SameValueZero for includes, -0 matching +0, a non-number never found); an
  index in count..length-1 is a hole and takes the generic lookup through the prototype chain.
  join/toString append each element's own number-to-string, so the text is the generic text.

  Copies. slice copies the run with one memcpy when the receiver is a plain Array with the default
  species and the range lies inside the elements; the result starts in the source's kind. splice uses
  the same copy for its result and then moves the elements in the raw storage.

  Moves. pop/shift move the tail down with memmove, reverse swaps in place, fill stores the value run
  in the kind (widening first for a non-int32 number), copyWithin is a memmove of the range,
  unshift/splice move the elements and store the inserted numbers.

  Concatenation. concat builds one packed result when the receiver and every argument are plain
  packed arrays of this realm with the default species and Symbol.isConcatSpreadable is untouched on
  the whole prototype chain of each of them (a Proxy or any other exotic object in a chain is
  refused): the widest kind and the total length are decided before any element is written, the
  result is reserved once and each piece is copied in. Anything else takes the generic route from the
  start.

  Iterators. The array iterator's next and the AOT for-of step read a packed element without
  unpacking, and the for-of element profiler records it.

  Construction and results. `[]` and `new Array(n)` start as the int32 kind, with n as the length and
  count 0, so indexes count..n-1 stay holes; map/filter/flatMap results that start packed keep their
  capacity in the packed kind.

Why we need it
  The packed kinds are only worth their complexity if the builtins keep arrays in them. Each helper
  here avoids a full unpack of the array (and a re-box of every element) on the paths a number-array
  program actually uses, and the AOT generator can emit typed loads on the storage these paths keep.

Hazards
  Widening, unpacking and reserving move the storage, so a site that has just called one re-reads
  p->u.array.u.values_raw and p->arr_kind (and the element count) instead of keeping a pointer.
  A callback, a getter, a Proxy trap or a species constructor can reach the array while a builtin
  runs, so a callback builtin re-reads the kind and the count before each iteration.
  A packed array holds no references: the mark function, the finalizer and the DRC scan skip its
  elements, and nothing of that kind stores a JSValue that needs a reference count.
  js_get_fast_array() returns false for a packed array, which keeps every JSValue-pointer path away
  from packed storage.
  A hole above the count (new Array(n) partly filled) is not a packed element: a read there goes to
  the prototype chain exactly as for a generic fast array.

Tests: .local/kit/packed1.js (species subclass, replaced Symbol.species, NaN/-0 equality, join
formatting, prototype getters in the hole range, callbacks that push/widen/shrink/grow, concat with
isConcatSpreadable and both kinds, fill widening); the 230-file torture set with QJS_PACKED_TEST=0
and 1; asan on packed1.js; the AOT unit for packed1 and the array kit kernels.
